<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hungarian Democratic Forum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1987">1987</a>–<a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MDF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hungarian Democratic Forum was a centre-right political party founded in 1987 at Lakitelek by intellectuals opposed to communist rule. It won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s first free elections in 1990, and its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozsef-antall">József Antall</a> became prime minister. After losing power in 1994 it declined steadily and dissolved itself in 2011.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Forum first met at Lakitelek in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 27 September 1987, during the one-party rule of the Hungarian Socialist Workers' Party, and was at first a loose movement rather than a party. Its founders, among them Sándor Csoóri, Zoltán Bíró, István Csurka and Sándor Lezsák, stood in the populist or rural-national tradition, sought a third way between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, and were troubled by the plight of the Hungarian minority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>. More moderate than the liberal Alliance of Free Democrats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidesz">Fidesz</a>, it sought links with the reform communist Imre Pozsgay.</p><p>At a second meeting in Lakitelek in 1988 the Forum announced its foundation, made possible by the Association and Assembly Act of September 1988, and in 1989 it took part in the Opposition Round Table and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-round-table-talks">National Round Table</a> talks. In October 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozsef-antall">József Antall</a> succeeded Zoltán Bíró as its president and opened the party to the national-minded and Christian middle class, turning it into a right-of-centre catch-all party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the elections of March and April 1990 the Forum won 24.7% of the vote and 164 of 386 seats and formed a coalition with the Independent Smallholders Party and the Christian Democratic People's Party. Antall's government passed laws on local administration, the civil service and the redress of the old regime's wrongs. A radical national faction called for former communists to be purged from public posts and attacked the liberal Free Democrats as cosmopolitan, liberal-bolshevik and Jewish, and in 1993 most of it left with István Csurka to found the Hungarian Justice and Life Party. After Antall's death in December 1993, Péter Boross led the government until May 1994.</p><p>The party fell to 12.0% in 1994 and split again in 1996, when centrists around Iván Szabó left. Joint candidates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidesz">Fidesz</a> gave it 17 seats in 1998, and it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viktor-orban">Viktor Orbán</a>'s coalition government until 2002. Led from 1999 to 2010 by Ibolya Dávid, it quarrelled with Fidesz, and in 2009 its MEP Lajos Bokros joined the European Conservatives and Reformists, which led to its suspension from the European People's Party. With Bokros as its candidate for prime minister it won only 2.67% in 2010 and left parliament for the first time; it was renamed Welfare and Freedom (JESZ) on 8 April 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hungarian Democratic Forum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Free elections in May 1990 brought the conservative <b>Hungarian Democratic Forum</b> to the head of a coalition, and József Antall became the first democratically elected prime minister since the war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
